From: Tiago Damascena <tiagobdamascena@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 12:00:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] Buttons by position, BUTTON LAYOUT per pad, the pad's own hotkey (RelicOS)

Observed on the device (M22 bench, builds 18-22): in the menu the
handheld confirmed with B and backed with A while an Xbox pad confirmed
with its A -- the ES confirms with the SOUTH button (Batocera's rule,
BUTTON_OK = "b"), and south is labelled B on this handheld and A on an
Xbox pad. And the ES's "x" had sat on the button printed Y on this
handheld since M14. Three options were tried on the bench and set aside
(BUTTONS BY LABEL, SWAP A AND B, SWAP X AND Y): whether a pad's letters
are known is the wrong question -- SDL names positions (a = south) and
learns letters only for Nintendo-brand pads; a PlayStation pad has none.
The only real choice is which position confirms, and those who move A
also move X: the two layouts are mirror images.

- The map is kept by POSITION for every pad: SDL is told not to use
  button labels (SDL_HINT_GAMECONTROLLER_USE_BUTTON_LABELS = 0) and the
  table's SNES swap applies to all -- SDL's a (south) is the ES's "b",
  SDL's b (east) the ES's "a", x/y likewise. One exception: the internal
  pad is a uinput device that names its top button BTN_NORTH/BTN_X as
  the kernel's gamepad documentation says, while SDL takes BTN_X for the
  west button as on an Xbox pad; its x/y are kept as SDL gives them, or
  the ES's "x" would sit on the button printed Y.

- A confirms and B backs (BUTTON_OK = ABUTTON, as on WIN32), on every
  pad; the menu's "switch confirm & cancel" still inverts.

- One option per pad, BUTTON LAYOUT (relicos.conf, controllers.<guid>.
  layout: empty = NINTENDO, A right, B bottom, the default on every pad
  and this handheld's own; "xbox" = A bottom, B right, the Xbox and
  PlayStation habit), applied at lookup time -- InputConfig::relicosName
  in getInputByName and isMappedTo -- so es_input.cfg is never rewritten.
  relicos-pads reads the same key for RetroArch. The page that sets it
  is the next patch's.

- "guide" maps to "hotkey": a pad's own Xbox/PS/Home button is its FN,
  which the launcher makes RetroArch's menu toggle. Without it the
  fallback made hotkey = select, and build 18 turned the Xbox's View
  button into both select and menu toggle.
---
diff -ruN a/es-core/src/InputConfig.cpp b/es-core/src/InputConfig.cpp
--- a/es-core/src/InputConfig.cpp	2026-09-14 17:16:46.862528397 -0300
+++ b/es-core/src/InputConfig.cpp	2026-09-14 17:17:16.768537615 -0300
@@ -3,6 +3,7 @@
 #include "Log.h"
 #include <pugixml/src/pugixml.hpp>
 #include "Settings.h"
+#include "SystemConf.h"
 #include "utils/StringUtil.h"
 
 #ifdef HAVE_UDEV
@@ -199,9 +200,42 @@
 		mNameMap.erase(it);
 }
 
+#ifdef RELICOS
+// The pad's BUTTON LAYOUT (CONTROLLER SETTINGS -> the pad, kept in
+// relicos.conf as controllers.<guid>.layout), applied here so that the
+// map on disk (es_input.cfg, by position: "a" is the right button, "b"
+// the bottom one, "x" the top, "y" the left, the SNES way) is never
+// rewritten and the menu and the games read the same choice:
+//   nintendo  (default, and the empty value) the map as it is: the
+//             right button confirms, as on a SNES or on this handheld.
+//   xbox      mirrored: a<->b and x<->y, so the bottom button confirms
+//             and the top one is Y -- the Xbox and PlayStation habit,
+//             on any pad, this handheld included.
+// Which physical button carries which letter is the pad's business:
+// SDL names positions (a = south) and RelicOS never asks for labels.
+// relicos-pads reads the same key for RetroArch.
+static std::string swapPair(const std::string& n, const char* p, const char* q)
+{
+	return n == p ? q : n == q ? p : n;
+}
+
+std::string InputConfig::relicosName(const std::string& name) const
+{
+	if (mDeviceId < 0 || (name != "a" && name != "b" && name != "x" && name != "y"))
+		return name;
+	if (SystemConf::getInstance()->get("controllers." + mDeviceGUID + ".layout") != "xbox")
+		return name;
+	return swapPair(swapPair(name, "a", "b"), "x", "y");
+}
+#endif
+
 bool InputConfig::getInputByName(const std::string& name, Input* result)
 {
+#ifdef RELICOS
+	auto it = mNameMap.find(relicosName(toLower(name)));
+#else
 	auto it = mNameMap.find(toLower(name));
+#endif
 	if(it != mNameMap.cend())
 	{
 		*result = it->second;
@@ -265,10 +299,17 @@
 
 		if (chk.device == input.device && chk.type == input.type && chk.id == input.id)
 		{
+#ifdef RELICOS
+			// The swaps are their own inverse: the stored name maps back
+			// to the name a caller would ask getInputByName for.
+			std::string mapped = relicosName(iterator->first);
+#else
+			std::string mapped = iterator->first;
+#endif
 			if (chk.type == TYPE_HAT)
 			{
 				if (input.value == 0 || input.value & chk.value)
-					maps.push_back(iterator->first);
+					maps.push_back(mapped);
 
 				continue;
 			}
@@ -276,10 +317,10 @@
 			if (input.type == TYPE_AXIS)
 			{
 				if (input.value == 0 || chk.value == input.value)
-					maps.push_back(iterator->first);
+					maps.push_back(mapped);
 			}
 			else
-				maps.push_back(iterator->first);
+				maps.push_back(mapped);
 		}
 	}
 
@@ -419,7 +460,10 @@
 {
 	bool invertButtons = Settings::getInstance()->getBool("InvertButtons");
 	
-#ifdef INVERTEDINPUTCONFIG
+#if defined(INVERTEDINPUTCONFIG) || defined(RELICOS)
+	// RelicOS: the ES's "a" is the button labelled A on whichever pad
+	// (see loadFromSdlMapping), and A confirms -- on the handheld and on
+	// a connected pad alike. The switch in the menu still inverts.
 	BUTTON_OK = invertButtons ? BBUTTON : ABUTTON;
 	BUTTON_BACK = invertButtons ? ABUTTON : BBUTTON;
 #else
diff -ruN a/es-core/src/InputConfig.h b/es-core/src/InputConfig.h
--- a/es-core/src/InputConfig.h	2026-09-14 17:16:46.862437202 -0300
+++ b/es-core/src/InputConfig.h	2026-09-14 17:17:16.768581624 -0300
@@ -128,6 +128,9 @@
 	inline const std::string& getDevicePath() { return mDevicePath; };
 
